98.735000,515.402000,48.020200,514.859000,298.712000,514.859000,48.020200,503.440000,298.712000,503.440000,48.020200,502.840000,298.701000,502.840000,48.020200,491.421000,298.701000,491.421000,48.020200,490.821000,298.691000,490.821000,48.020200,479.402000,298.691000,479.402000,48.020200,478.859000,298.709000,478.859000,48.020200,467.440000,298.709000,467.440000,48.020200,466.840000,298.744000,466.840000,48.020200,455.422000,298.744000,455.422000,48.020200,454.821000,298.708000,454.821000,48.020200,443.402000,298.708000,443.402000,48.020200,442.859000,298.701000,442.859000,48.020200,431.440000,298.701000,431.440000,48.020200,430.840000,298.745000,430.840000,48.020200,419.421000,298.745000,419.421000,48.020200,418.822000,148.173000,418.822000,48.020200,407.403000,148.173000,407.403000" rect="47.662800,407.046000,301.015000,743.177000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Digitisation accuracy appeared to be improved by applying&#xD;black body paint to body landmarks compared with markerless&#xD;analysis methods (Ceseracciu et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2011</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>; Cronin et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2019</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>). In&#xD;regards to the NNs trained in DeepLabCut</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-8.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>TM </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>for an aquatic&#xD;setting, the use of painted landmarks improved 2D digitisation&#xD;error from 10 mm to 4–5 mm in our study (Cronin et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2019</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>).&#xD;Additional time and expertise, however, is required to mark&#xD;swimmers. Sports scientists and coaches should consider the&#xD;trade-off between preparation time and accuracy when using&#xD;NNs to digitise 2D video. The methods presented here could be&#xD;used in future research involving kinematic analysis of landbased&#xD;activities, especially those performed predominantly in&#xD;a single plane of motion. In athletics, for instance, a fixedcamera&#xD;setup and pre-calibrated area could be used to assess&#xD;2D kinematics of running, jumping, or throwing in a training&#xD;environment. Kinematic analysis in weightlifting commonly&#xD;involves video and manual digitisation methods to estimate&#xD;barbell trajectory during lifts (Garhammer, </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>1985</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>). Barbell trajectory&#xD;can be used to assess movement characteristics, provide&#xD;technical feedback, and calculate critical performance variables,&#xD;such as barbell velocity (Garhammer &amp; Newton, </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2013</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>).&#xD;Automated digitisation of the end of the barbell in 2D video,&#xD;however, is difficult as it can exhibit similar colour characteristics&#xD;to the surrounding image (Dahlin et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2017</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>). Given the maximal&#xD;barbell velocity of elite weightlifters during the snatch lift is&#xD;between 1.5–2 m/s (Gourgoulis et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2009</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>; Nagao et al., </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007F;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2019</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>),&#xD;NNs could be used for automated digitisation of the barbell in&#xD;the sport of weightlifting.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
